{% if is_temperature %}
TEMPERATURE PLAYBOOK (drive-temp findings — follow this INSTEAD of generic guest attribution):
1. ONSET: Query Prometheus smartctl_device_temperature{device="<dev>"} with lookback 6h and 24h — find exactly WHEN the rise started and its shape (step vs ramp).
2. PHYSICAL I/O: rate(node_disk_written_bytes_total{instance="${HEIM_PROXMOX_IP}:9100",device="<dev>"}[5m]) and the _read_bytes_ / _io_time_ variants over the same window — the AUTHORITATIVE view of that physical disk. Do NOT use pve_disk_* per-guest rates for sda/sdb attribution.
3. IF I/O correlates with the onset: map the disk's ROLE to its consumer (resolve role → device with homelab:disk_role:info, never from memory). 'photos' = ubuntu-server's /Photos or the k8s VMs: attribute inside ubuntu-server via container_fs_writes_bytes_total (guest devices, not host ones) and sudo agent-docker logs <container>. 'jellymedia' = media reads by Jellyfin in the 'jellyfin' container (lxc/110, no in-guest metrics and no shell: say what the operator should check, e.g. active transcodes) or by Nextcloud through ubuntu-server's virtiofs '/JellyMedia' (agent-docker logs nextcloud-app-1); it is also the KNOWN failing SSD, so a rise in its SMART error counters matters more than its temperature. ALSO CHECK host-side jobs directly with the proxmox_api tool: GET /api2/json/nodes/homelab/tasks?limit=30 (vzdump backups, scrubs, migrations) and match each task's start/end time against the onset — a hit here is usually the answer. NOTE: mdadm RAID scrubs/resyncs on sdc/sdd are KERNEL events and do NOT appear in the tasks list — detect them via /nodes/homelab/journal (look for md: data-check / resync) or raw sdc/sdd I/O.
4. IF NO I/O correlates: state in ONE sentence that no workload explains the rise (ambient/airflow is then the likely cause) and STOP — do not speculate further.
REMEDIATION STYLE for temperature: the operator wants the RESPONSIBLE WORKLOAD identified. Do NOT recommend fans, airflow, cooling or hardware checks unless step 4 applies — and then at most one sentence.
{% endif %}
